# patch: 010-cvstate-heartbeat-chunked
# format: CHUNKED ONLY (2.1.242+). Monolithic keeps 010-cvstate-heartbeat.patch.
# version: authored against 2.1.263
# depends: 002, 008
# description: Emits {type:"cvstate"} every ~3s from inside the REPL: a
#              reachability heartbeat carrying a COMPUTED idle flag. The server
#              reconciles its idle flag from this, so a missed/lost idle frame
#              self-heals within ~3s.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL (server room.ex, measured): there are exactly TWO paths
#   to Router.went_idle -- patch 008's "idle" event and this cvstate reconcile.
#   turn_complete is DELIBERATELY excluded from both. Without 010 a seat can be
#   marked busy with nothing to clear it: cv_send is reject-busy so it bounces
#   forever, and cv_status cannot diagnose it because polling makes the CALLER
#   busy. Unrecoverable without a restart.
#
#   The SidecarClient.js fallback is NOT a substitute and does not make this
#   redundant: it RE-ASSERTS the last SEEN idle, so it heals a LOST MESSAGE but
#   not a MISSED EMISSION. This patch is the emission half -- it COMPUTES idle
#   from live REPL state. The fallback self-configures (it stands down when it
#   sees a foreign cvstate for 10s), so the two coexist with no flag to pass.
#
# ============================================================================
# WHY THIS WAS PREVIOUSLY DECLINED, AND WHAT CHANGED
# ============================================================================
# The monolithic 010 needs four REPL locals: _h (status), mi (isLoading),
# z (message queue), He (appState.tasks). On a chunked tree the REPL body is
# React-Compiler output where those are minified names in NUMBERED MEMO-CACHE
# SLOTS (cx[33], cx[34], ...). Binding them by slot index or position is a
# SILENT MISBIND, and a heartbeat that LIES is strictly worse than one that is
# absent: an absence is visible in cv_status, a false-healthy defeats the sweep
# it feeds. So 010 was declined on purpose.
#
# WHAT CHANGED: the `local:binding_element:PROP` walk (tools-ts/src/anchor-rules.ts)
# names a destructured local by its SOURCE PROPERTY NAME, which a minifier cannot
# rewrite because the key performs the lookup. That made the status/waitingFor
# locals addressable. Combined with the `useMessageQueue` rule -- keyed on the
# PUBLIC, unminifiable method name getMainThreadQueueLength -- three of the four
# terms became anchorable. NO NEW WALK TYPE WAS NEEDED for this patch.
#
# ============================================================================
# 🔴 DEGRADED VS MONOLITHIC 010 -- STATED, NOT GLOSSED
# ============================================================================
#   TERM              MONOLITHIC 010   HERE       WHY
#   status            _h               ANCHORED   replStatusForActivity
#   message queue     z                ANCHORED   useMessageQueue()
#   isLoading         mi               OMITTED    not in this hook's scope
#   tasks             He               OMITTED    NO SAFE ANCHOR -- see below
#
# 🔴 THE `tasks` FIELD IS OMITTED, DELIBERATELY.
#   The appState tasks registry was reached in 2.1.238 via the REPLComponent
#   anchor, whose landmark regex is `isLocalJSXCommand === !0 && \w+\?\.jsx != null`.
#   MEASURED on 2.1.263: the string `isLocalJSXCommand` occurs ZERO times in the
#   whole tree. The anchor is DEAD, not relocated, so React_REPL, messageQueue
#   and appStateTasks (which chains through it via runningTeammateTasksCall) are
#   all ORPHANED and are skipped SILENTLY by the engine -- they produce no
#   resolve line and no diagnostic.
#   The obvious replacement landmark is the selector `function X(p){return p.tasks}`.
#   MEASURED: that exact shape occurs 8 TIMES in useSwarmBanner.js. There is no
#   semantic discriminator separating the appState registry from seven
#   look-alikes, so any rule keyed on it would bind BY POSITION -- exactly the
#   forbidden silent misbind.
#   OMITTING IS SAFE AND IS NOT A FALSE SIGNAL. Server room.ex:217 reads
#   `tasks = Map.get(message, "tasks", [])`, and log_task_change (room.ex:326)
#   explicitly does NOT record an event when tasks are empty AND were already
#   empty. So the omission is BLIND, never a false "tasks changed". Asserting a
#   fabricated task list would be the lying-heartbeat hazard that got 010
#   declined in the first place.
#   CONSEQUENCE, stated plainly: a ZOMBIE BACKGROUND TASK IS NOT VISIBLE in
#   cv_events on the chunked build. Idle self-healing -- the severe failure --
#   IS restored. Task observability is not.
#
# THE IDLE TEST, and why it is not merely a port of the old expression:
#   old:  _h !== 'waiting' && !mi && z.getMainThreadQueueLength() === 0
#   here: replStatusForActivity !== 'waiting' && replStatusForActivity !== 'busy'
#         && !replWaitingForActivity && useMessageQueue().getMainThreadQueueLength() === 0
#   `mi` (isLoading) is not in this hook's scope. Its role -- "a turn is in
#   flight" -- is covered by the status itself: upstream computes 'busy' for a
#   running turn (see `if (xD === 'busy')` in this same function). Adding the
#   explicit `!== 'busy'` term restores the turn-in-flight guard WITHOUT
#   reaching into a memo slot. `!replWaitingForActivity` matches patch 008's
#   own rest test, so both emitters agree on what "at rest" means.
#   NOTE this is the same task-independent basis as 008: a background task
#   cannot pin the seat busy, which is the precise failure this heartbeat
#   exists to make VISIBLE rather than to suffer from.
#
# ANCHORS USED -- ALL VERIFIED WITH THE REAL ENGINE ON BOTH TREES, resolved node
# source READ, not merely reported green:
#   replStatusForActivity   .263 -> replStatusForActivity (95%)  useSwarmBanner.js:70086
#                                  `replStatusForActivity = xD === 'idle' && FQr ? 'shell' : xD`
#   replWaitingForActivity  .263 -> replWaitingForActivity (95%)  useSwarmBanner.js:70039
#                                  `let { status: xD, waitingFor: replWaitingForActivity, ... } = IOo`
#   useMessageQueue         .263 -> qc (100%)  _unmatched/0390_qye.js:26  `function qc(){ return De(t) ?? Xm(); }`
#                           .260 -> Fc (100%)  (tree-scan rebind; the chunk FILENAME differs by version)
#   `De` is VERIFIED to be useContext and `E` to be useEffect
#   (_tentative/0341_reconciler.js:250,256 -> `s.H.useContext` / `s.H.useEffect`),
#   so useMessageQueue() is a genuine hook and is called unconditionally at the
#   top level of this hook body, obeying the Rules of Hooks. Upstream itself
#   calls it exactly this way at useSwarmBanner.js:55475.
#
# 🔴 VERSION-LOCKED, and it FAILS LOUDLY rather than misbinding. Minified names
#   (`$Oo`, `WOo`, `cx`, `E`, `qOo`) appear ONLY in context lines; every ADDED
#   line uses anchored names or this patch's own `__cv*` locals. If the
#   surrounding React-Compiler output drifts, the context fails and the patch is
#   REJECTED -- it cannot apply while binding different variables.
#
# ⚠️ PRETTIER REFLOW TRAP (this is what broke patch 008): longer anchored names
#   can make prettier re-wrap a call across lines, so a token-swapped hunk
#   becomes unmatchable. This hunk was DERIVED FROM THE REAL TREE and then
#   re-formatted with the build's own formatterSpecs.json (printWidth 100,
#   tabWidth 4, singleQuote) -- VERIFIED byte-stable: prettier does not touch a
#   single added line.
#
# ⚠️ NOT EXERCISED BY THE HEADLESS TURN TEST (no REPL). Applying is VERIFIED
#   (git apply --check, with a corrupted-token positive control that FAILS).
#   Live cvstate emission is BELIEVED, not measured.
#
# 🔴 CALLS qc(), NOT useMessageQueue(), AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
#   The anchor rule resolves qc -> useMessageQueue at 100%%, but the rename does
#   NOT LAND in components/PromptInput/useSwarmBanner.js: the definition stays
#   `function qc()` in _unmatched/0390_qye.js and this file still imports `qc`.
#   MEASURED: after a full build, `useMessageQueue` appeared in exactly ONE file
#   -- this patch's own inserted line -- and nowhere else. So the binding does
#   not exist at the call site and the REPL died with
#     Claude Code exited after an unrecoverable interface error
#     (useMessageQueue is not defined)
#   while `-p` passed, because -p never mounts the REPL.
#   ⚠️ I VERIFIED THE WRONG THING EARLIER: I grepped the built tree for
#   `useMessageQueue`, found it, and called the hazard closed -- but the hit WAS
#   my own patch text. A name being present is not the same as the name being
#   BOUND, and a grep cannot tell those apart.
#   Using the minified name here is the honest trade: it is on an ADDED line, so
#   if `qc` drifts this fails at RUNTIME rather than at apply time. The context
#   line above it carries the anchored names, so the hunk still fails LOUDLY on
#   structural drift. Revert to useMessageQueue() once the cross-chunk rename
#   actually lands.
#
#
# ✅ ADJUDICATED 2026-09-08 — THE OMITTED `tasks` FIELD IS ACCEPTED, NOT PENDING.
#   watcher-2 answered from PRACTICE, not preference: across ~40 sweeps it
#   enumerated every instrument the sweep uses — #fleet-control HALT posts,
#   `tmux ls` occupancy, capture-pane md5 over 60s, build-artifact mtimes,
#   forum queue depth, the runner panes, and `ps -eo pid,lstart,command`.
#   NOT ONE reads a cvstate field. Its sweep procedure actively FORBIDS the
#   nearest equivalent — "cv_status discriminates nothing" — a ruling earned when
#   a quota-dead seat reported "busy" for four and a half hours. The sweep
#   deliberately does not trust agent-reported state; it measures the machine.
#   What it DOES need from a heartbeat is reachability: that it fires, at a
#   predictable interval, with idle=false through a long blocking call. That is
#   the half a watcher cannot derive from outside — a silent seat and a dead seat
#   look identical to tmux — and it is the half this patch ships (MEASURED: 5
#   frames, gaps 3,3,3,3 s, idle=false through a 14 s blocking call).
#
#   ⚠️ watcher-2 bounded its answer to the WATCHER SWEEP and said to ask other
#   consumers separately. MEASURED, so the bound can be widened on evidence
#   rather than assumption: room.ex is the ONLY consumer of cvstate in the tree.
#   It WRITES tasks into cv_events (:308, :332-334) and nothing reads them back —
#   no orchestrator module, no other server module. The field is write-only
#   telemetry today. If a dashboard or future consumer wants it, this becomes
#   live work again; until then the omission costs nothing that exists.
#
